sed some exposition  that i was meant to remember  as technobabble and that's not great now  the good side of technobabble is that  it's incredibly immersive with this book  if anything it leaves you with a  distinct impression of what this world  is like i  left neuromancer with a real feel and  vibe for william gibson's world that he  has created  one that i wanted to spend more time in  and that's great but the pros doesn't  flow as well  as it could have there's also a lot of  switching between  time and place different times different  places different perspectives  and you can sort of do it like that on a  flip of a  dime and i spoke with my friend benji  from the tail foundry who also really  enjoyed this book and he agreed that we  both got a little bit lost around the 75  percent mark  in terms of knowing why people are where  they  are and what they're doing there  there's that all comes back to that  technobowl stuff is that you kind of  miss  important points of exposition because  you dismiss it along with  so much of the other information you're  trying to process but gibson does manage  to bring this all together at the end  and you do kind of understand how it all  ends up and why the characters are fine  i mean but this is a trait of older  sci-fi as it is the  characters of case molly armitage i  didn't get quite a  great sense of who they were but i did  really good good scenes of wintermute  who is a major character in the book but  i mean  don't listen to me what do i know this  book won the arthur c clarke  hugo and nebula awards all on its own so  clearly it was doing something  incredible  uh at the time and maybe i just didn't  get it maybe i'm  i'm just not smart enough but i still  really did enjoy neuromancer and i  didn't want to leave this iconic world  that gibson created however  if you want to get into the cyberpunk  genre if you want to read his work  then i'm not going to suggest  neuromancer i'm going to suggest  burning chrome th